## 3. Technology & Innovation Frameworks

LEGO’s success is rooted in material science and engineering innovations that support open‑ended play.  Key technological milestones and patents include:

### 3.1 Early Innovations (1930s–1950s)

- **Wooden Toys & Quality Focus (1930s):** Ole Kirk Kristiansen emphasised high‑quality beechwood and varnish for his wooden toys, establishing the company motto “Only the best is good enough”【973641136094341†L54-L60】.
- **First Plastic Injection‑Molding Machine (1946 – 1947):** Post‑war scarcity of quality wood led Kristiansen to invest in a British plastic injection‑molding machine, arriving in Billund in December 1947【187948212495207†L4-L16】.  This investment allowed experimentation with cellulose acetate and marked LEGO’s entry into the plastic age.
- **Automatic Binding Bricks (1949):** Early plastic bricks called Automatic Binding Bricks were released in 1949; while rudimentary, they demonstrated the potential of interlocking bricks【187948212495207†L24-L35】.  Initially, critics viewed plastics as risky, but Kristiansen argued that global success was possible【187948212495207†L34-L35】.
- **LEGO Mursten & Branding (1951 – 1953):** The name “Automatic Binding Bricks” was replaced with **LEGO Mursten**; the LEGO name was molded into each brick starting in 1953【187948212495207†L45-L51】.
- **Stud‑and‑Tube Patent (1958):** Market feedback about unstable hollow bricks prompted Godtfred Kirk Christiansen to design the stud‑and‑tube system.  LEGO submitted a patent application on 28 January 1958 for “a toy building element”【344533715736306†L16-L26】.  The design dramatically improved clutch power and provided nearly a billion combination possibilities for six 2×4 bricks【344533715736306†L16-L26】.

### 3.2 System Expansion & Minifigures (1960s–1970s)

- **Wheel & Train (1961 – 1966):** The LEGO wheel (1961) and train system (1966) extended play possibilities, enabling vehicles and train layouts (not covered in official articles but widely acknowledged in product history).  The development of new elements emphasised the **LEGO System in Play** concept: unlimited combinations across a family of compatible parts.
- **DUPLO (1969):** To extend the age range downward, LEGO introduced DUPLO bricks in 1969.  These larger bricks maintain compatibility via hollow studs and were patented in 1967; the global launch came in 1969【682976293639197†L35-L41】.
- **LEGO® Technic & Expert Builder (late 1970s):** Technic sets introduced gears, axles and pneumatic components, challenging older children to build models with functional mechanisms.  While the exact launch year (1977) is widely noted by historians, official LEGO sources focus on subsequent innovations rather than the launch date.
- **Minifigure (1978):** Danish designer Jens Nygård Knudsen refined earlier figures into the modern **LEGO minifigure**, a character with movable arms and legs that fits perfectly into the LEGO system.  Management approved the design, and the minifigure launched in 1978【482968692967916†L45-L51】.  Its addition transformed LEGO sets from static constructions into narrative worlds.

### 3.3 Robotics & Digital Play (1980s–2000s)

- **LEGO Education & Institutional Department (1980):** Under CEO Kjeld Kirk Kristiansen, LEGO established an educational division to explore the brick as a learning material【650828966092506†L9-L16】.  This department evolved through names (DACTA, LEGO Educational Division) before becoming **LEGO Education** in 2006【650828966092506†L36-L39】.
- **Collaborations with MIT & Seymour Papert (1980s):** Inspired by the LOGO programming language, Kjeld Kirk Kristiansen initiated a partnership with the MIT Media Lab.  Early programmable products like **LEGO Technic Control I** (mid‑1980s) paved the way for future robotics kits【650828966092506†L93-L115】.
- **MINDSTORMS Robotics Invention System (1998):** The most notable result of the MIT collaboration, MINDSTORMS introduced a programmable brick (RCX), sensors and motors.  The kit reached millions of people and formed the basis for the FIRST LEGO League competitions【650828966092506†L93-L115】.  Later iterations (NXT, EV3) and the SPIKE Prime system continue this legacy, blending engineering with coding education.

### 3.4 Sustainability & Material Science (2010s–present)

- **Plant‑Based Elements (2018):** LEGO introduced elements made from **bio‑polyethylene (bio‑PE)** derived from sugarcane.  More than 200 different elements—such as leaves and minifigure accessories—are produced using bio‑PE【893787380151024†L55-L61】.  The company works with WWF to ensure the sugarcane sources meet sustainability criteria【893787380151024†L55-L61】.
- **Recycled Transparent Elements (2024):** New transparent elements contain 20 % recycled materials derived from artificial marble kitchen worktops, known as **arMABS** plastics【893787380151024†L68-L71】.  Over 900 different arMABS elements are expected to appear in 85 % of sets【893787380151024†L68-L71】.
- **CO₂‑based Plastics (ePOM, 2026):** LEGO plans to adopt **ePOM**, a plastic created from bio‑waste and renewable energy, for robust elements like axles by 2026【893787380151024†L78-L80】.  This initiative exemplifies LEGO’s investment in cutting‑edge material science.
- **Corporate Targets:** The LEGO Group aims to produce bricks from sustainable materials by 2032 and reduce absolute greenhouse‑gas emissions by 37 % compared with 2019, with a longer‑term goal of net‑zero emissions by 2050【87120999531522†L36-L49】.  The company also pledges to eliminate single‑use plastic in packaging and invest in renewable energy【87120999531522†L46-L51】.

